Chapter 3: Fixed Sliding Window

This is the easiest version.

The window size never changes.

Examples:

Maximum sum of 5 elements
Average of K numbers
Maximum score in K cards
First negative number in every window

Window Size

Always K
Code Pattern
int sum = 0;

// First window
for(int i = 0; i < k; i++)
    sum += nums[i];

int ans = sum;

// Slide
for(int i = k; i < nums.size(); i++)
{
    sum += nums[i];
    sum -= nums[i-k];

    ans = max(ans, sum);
}

Notice

sum += nums[i];

New element enters.

sum -= nums[i-k];

Old element leaves.

Chapter 4: Window Analogy

Imagine a train with exactly 3 seats.

Passengers:

A B C D E F

Initially

[A B C]

Move forward

A [B C D]

One passenger leaves.

One passenger enters.

The train still has 3 seats.

Exactly what Sliding Window does.

Chapter 5: Variable Sliding Window

Now things get interesting.

Question:

Find the longest substring without repeating characters.

Can we keep window size fixed?

No.

Sometimes

abc

Sometimes

abcdef

Sometimes

ab

Window size changes.

We keep

Left Pointer (L)

Right Pointer (R)

Initially

L
↓

a b c d
↑
R

Move Right

a b c d
  ↑
  R

If everything is valid,

expand.

If condition breaks,

move Left until valid again.

Chapter 6: Variable Window Example

Question:

Longest subarray with sum ≤ 10

Array

2 3 4 5 1

Start

[2]

Sum = 2

Expand

[2 3]

Sum = 5

Expand

[2 3 4]

Sum = 9

Expand

[2 3 4 5]

Sum = 14 ❌

Too big.

Shrink from left.

Remove

2

Now

[3 4 5]

Still 12 ❌

Remove

3

Now

[4 5]

Sum = 9 ✅

Continue expanding.

Variable Window Template
int left = 0;

for(int right = 0; right < n; right++)
{
    // Include nums[right]

    while(condition is broken)
    {
        // Remove nums[left]
        left++;
    }

    // Update answer
}

You'll use this exact template dozens of times.

Chapter 7: Fixed vs Variable
Fixed Window	Variable Window
Size always K	Size changes
One loop after building first window	Two pointers
Easier	More common in interviews
Questions mention K	Questions mention longest, shortest, at most, no duplicates
Chapter 8: When Should Your Brain Think "Sliding Window"?

Whenever the problem mentions:

✅ Consecutive elements

✅ Continuous subarray

✅ Continuous substring

✅ Window of size K

✅ Longest substring

✅ Shortest subarray

✅ At most K

✅ Exactly K

These are huge clues.

